{"kind":"report","text":"approved at exact clean pushed head ce30731f25202470a3d31b051f4103399deb0209, naming all four artifacts: 1fd55a87 at cmd/chess, 97da8d80 at README.md, c158cc8b at go.mod and 540ccdea at go.sum. This is the half a person can actually use, so I reviewed it as a recovery path for real data rather than as a CLI addition.\n\nAUTHORITY AND KEY CUSTODY, the thing I said I would be hardest on. Holds, and I read the code myself rather than accepting the report. runRebind calls readKey(store), not ensureKey, which is the only key-generating path in the binary. The doc comment states the reason better than I would have: losing the initializing key is not authority to invent its replacement. That is exactly right, and it is the difference between a recovery command and a way to claim a repository you do not own. The comparison is against log.Records[0].ActorKey, the first record's actor key, which is an immutable log fact rather than a mutable role or a current config value. A missing key wraps as a read error, and the test asserts no key file appears afterwards.\n\nREFUSALS PRECEDE THE PROMPT, which I asked for specifically because refusing after a prompt teaches a person to confirm past warnings. Holds, and I verified it by code order rather than by the presence of the refusals. readableOlderChessLog runs first, then openKeyStore and readKey, then the initializing-actor comparison, and only then confirmRebind. So an already-current binding, an unknown binding, a missing key and a foreign key all refuse before a single prompt byte is written. Two tests assert output length is zero on the wrong-signer and already-current paths, which pins the property rather than describing it.\n\nADMISSION IS BOUNDED. The supported set is the literal list chess-fold@1 and chess-fold@0, and anything else refuses with a message naming what it could not do. An already-current repository refuses before any key work. Opening under the outgoing identity first also verifies the sequence without weakening host.Open's exact binding rule, which is a neat way to get verification without a second code path.\n\nTHE WARNING IS HONEST. It names both versions and states the consequence: records in this repository were folded under the outgoing fold, and after rebinding some records may be interpreted differently. That is the sentence I wanted. It does not merely say the action is irreversible, which would tell a person nothing about what they are agreeing to. Confirmation requires typing rebind exactly, is bounded at 33 bytes, and refuses on EOF or anything else.\n\nEXACTLY ONE RECORD AND NOTHING ELSE MUTATED, the claim I most wanted independently confirmed. Holds, and the test is stronger than I expected. assertGitObjectsUnchanged walks git rev-list --objects --all and captures type and full contents per object identifier, then requires every prior identifier to still exist byte-identical. Because records are content-addressed objects reachable from all refs, a history rewrite would surface as a disappeared or changed object. Depth increases by exactly one and the final record carries the binding schema. That is object-level immutability rather than the weaker it-still-opens.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE, reproduced under this review rather than cited. Making the initializing-signer comparison always succeed produces exactly one failure, TestRebindRefusesNonInitializingSignerWithoutPrompting, and it fails on the refuse-before-prompt property with the prompt text in the assertion. The tree returned clean at the exact head afterwards. That is the right seam: core still refuses the append itself, so the chess-side check exists to refuse early and the test pins precisely that.\n\nDEFENCE IN DEPTH, worth stating because it changes how severe a chess-side mistake would be. host.ReplaceBinding independently re-verifies the signer against the first record, both before submit and again inside the submitter's PostDedup hook on every CAS attempt. So breaking the chess check cannot mint authority; it can only break the early-refusal contract. I approved that core behaviour and it is doing its job here.\n\nFAILURE MODES. A racing rebind or a repository moving underneath is caught by the PostDedup recheck, which refuses when the binding in force is no longer the expected outgoing one while tolerating ordinary records appended in between. A partial append cannot leave a half state: the submitter lands the record or errors. And the case I would most expect to be glossed is handled honestly, with the message that the binding was replaced but this build cannot open the repository.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Chess stays in the replaceable application layer, consuming only exported host surface. No contract moves here; the binding-replacement authority path was documented in core's architecture page in the same core head that shipped it. The dependency bump is what it claims: go.mod moves to the pseudo-version for fd5147a9c, which is the ReplaceBinding merge and an ancestor of core main, go.sum is consistent, and go mod verify passes.\n\nSECURITY. No unresolved defect. Key custody reuses the hardened path with os.Root pinning, symlink refusal and mode enforcement; recovery never generates a key; the signer check binds to an immutable first-record fact rather than a revocable role, which is the property that matters when authority is checked over an interval rather than an instant. Inputs are bounded: 33-byte confirmation, size-limited key read, a closed two-element fold list. No secrets in flags or output.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. One honest observation rather than a change I would block on. The discarded application.Fold(log) call is commented as proving this build can read the target record stream, but Fold is total and represents malformed acts as refusals rather than errors, so the call can only catch a panic. It proves less than the comment claims. Either drop it or say plainly that it is a panic smoke-check. Not worth delaying a user-recovery path.\n\nONE THING FOR THE MERGE, not a defect in this head. Its base is 0ccbeb76 rather than current chess main c35be0c9, so the diff against main looks alarming but is main-side work the branch predates. merge-tree against c35be0c9 is clean, and codex verified that read-only before I reviewed. But merge-clean is not gate-green: the combined result, this head plus main's node UI gate and display names, has never been tested together, and main now requires node for the Go suite. Run the full suite after the merge, not only before it.\n\nGATES at the exact head, in a clean detached worktree, local and upstream and the actual remote all equal: gofmt silent, go build and go vet clean, git diff --check clean, go mod verify all modules verified, the focused TestRebind set passing, and one full go test ./... -count=1 run with both packages ok. One suite, not two, on a loaded machine.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:1b96687964cbba5a6089c526d7136b32ba9488a5#git:sha1:1fd55a876b0c41ac8f1d9fb2f674536ef8f57ade","head":"ce30731f25202470a3d31b051f4103399deb0209","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:1b96687964cbba5a6089c526d7136b32ba9488a5#git:sha1:98ce168c773eec794f6dc772fe7dd6d243ea3cbc\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:1b96687964cbba5a6089c526d7136b32ba9488a5#git:sha1:98ce168c773eec794f6dc772fe7dd6d243ea3cbc","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}